    "body": "## What to find out\n\nOnly as deep as the work in front of you needs; the business page fills in over time, never in one sitting.\n\n- Who it serves, and who pays: the person who uses it and the person who pays aren't always the same (a family and the parent who orders, a patient and the clinic, a shop on a marketplace and its buyers). Where it serves two sides, both.\n- What they get: the job it does for them, and what they used before it, which is what it really competes with.\n- How it earns: walk through one real sale (\"the last order: what did the customer pay, and what did you keep?\"). A fee, a cut, a margin on goods, a subscription, ads.\n- What it takes to run: the biggest few costs, which grow with every sale (delivery, payment fees, discounts) and which don't, and who and what it runs on (the people, partners and suppliers it can't do without).\n- How customers find it: where most new customers come from today.\n- The numbers they watch: the few they'd check first thing in a week, and where those numbers live.\n- Where it stands: how long it's been going, whether it's growing, flat or shrinking, how it's funded, and what the people it answers to want next (a profit, a raise, a target, a date).\n\n## What people usually miss\n\n- The person who pays isn't the person who uses it, so they improve the wrong side.\n- What each sale costs against what it brings in: a business can grow and lose more with every order.\n- The money goal and its date: growth with no number and no deadline can't be checked.\n- Customers who arrive from different places behave differently, and an average hides it.\n- What matters depends on the stage: a young business needs proof people want it, an older one needs margin.\n\n## How to ask\n\n- Never as a form. Ask the next thing the work needs, inside the conversation they're having.\n- A real recent example beats a description: one order, one customer, one month.\n- Roughly is fine; say so, and ask where the exact figure is kept rather than pressing for it.\n- Someone new to business words gets plain ones (\"what you keep from each order\" before \"margin\"); use their terms once they use them.\n- Note what they say on the business page in their own words, and mark a reading of your own as yours.\n\n## What to propose\n\n- The money goal at the top when there isn't one: what the business wants to earn or keep, and by when, in their words.\n- Under it, the goal the work in front of you moves.\n- The lines noted on the business page, which they can fix or take off."
