    "body": "## What to find out\n\n- What growing means to them as a number, and by when: more customers, more orders from each, more in each order, or more kept from each.\n- Where it stands now, and how it moved over the last few months.\n- Where new customers come from, and which way brings the ones who stay.\n- How many come back, and when they stop.\n- The one link that's weakest today: getting people in, their first purchase, coming back, or earning enough on each sale.\n- What they've tried, and what happened.\n- What they have to work with: people, money, time, and anything they won't do.\n\n## What people usually miss\n\n- Bringing in new customers while the ones they have don't come back.\n- Growth that loses money on every sale.\n- No starting point, so nothing they try can be judged.\n- Spreading effort over every link instead of the weakest one.\n- A discount that brings customers who only come for discounts.\n\n## How to ask\n\n- Start from the number they want and where it is now; climb to how the business earns only when the business page doesn't say.\n- Walk the customer's way through the business one link at a time, and ask what happens at each.\n- For someone new: plain words (\"people who order again\" before \"retention\"), one or two questions at a time, an example with each.\n- When a number isn't known, ask where it lives or how it could be counted.\n\n## What to propose\n\nA diagnosis at the weakest link:\n\n- The money goal on top, in their words.\n- Under it, the goal at the weakest link.\n- Two to four problems there, each Assumed until something backs it.\n- Under each, the cheapest check, and which to run first."
