    "body": "## What to find out\n\n- The problem in their words, and one specific person who has it, ideally someone they know by name.\n- The last time that person ran into it: what happened, and what it cost them in time, money or worry.\n- What they do about it today, and what's wrong with that. Where they do nothing, why it hasn't been worth doing.\n- Why now: what changed that makes it possible or needed.\n- Why them: what they know, who they know or what they can do that others can't.\n- How they'd reach the first few people who have it.\n- What would show those people want it: a commitment (money, time, an introduction), never a compliment.\n- Who would pay, and roughly how.\n\n## What people usually miss\n\n- Building before talking to the people who have the problem.\n- Describing a solution as if it were the problem (\"people need an app for…\").\n- Everyone as the audience: the first users are a small, specific group.\n- Asking friends whether they like the idea, and counting their yes.\n- An idea many have tried that looks easy, with a reason it keeps failing that isn't visible from outside.\n\n## How to ask\n\n- About the past and real people, never \"would you use…\".\n- One thing at a time for someone new, with an example of a useful answer.\n- When they don't know something about the people who have the problem, that's the first thing to find out, not a reason to stop: say how they could find out.\n- Once the problem and the people are clear, offer to read what people say about it online, and say what that would add.\n\n## What to propose\n\nA diagnosis around learning, not a product:\n\n- A learning goal: finding out whether the people who have the problem want it solved enough to act.\n- The problems as they understand them, each Assumed until people's own words back it.\n- Under each, the cheapest check: talk to a handful of people who had the problem lately, ask for a commitment (a pre-order, a deposit, their time), or do the service by hand for a few people before building anything.\n- Which check to run first, and why that one."
