    "body": "## What to find out\n\nWork outward from the moment they named, only as far as the diagnosis needs:\n\n- What happens there from the customer's side: what people came to that step to do, and what they do instead.\n- What made them look at it now: the last time they noticed something wrong there, what they saw, and who told them.\n- How they know: something they counted, something people said (customers, staff, the team), or a feeling. Never treat a feeling as a measure, and never wave it away either.\n- How big it is, where they have any count at all: how many reach the step and how many get past it, before and now. Where they have none, find out where it could be counted (their analytics, their orders, their payment provider, a spreadsheet) instead of asking for a guess.\n- When it started, and what changed around then: a price, a fee or a minimum shown at that step, a new version, stock or availability, a new way customers arrive, the season, something a competitor did.\n- Who has it: everyone, or one kind of customer (new or returning, one area, one device, one way of paying).\n- What people do instead: leave, come back later, call, buy somewhere else.\n- What the step does for the business: which number moves when it works (orders, the size of each order, people coming back) and, in a line, how the business earns on it. Climb no further than that to anchor a goal.\n- What they've tried already, and what happened.\n\n## What people usually miss\n\n- A change outside the page that shows up on the page: a price, a fee, a minimum, a delivery time or stock that people first see there.\n- The symptom taken for the cause: where people leave is where it shows, not why they leave.\n- No before and after: a drop is only a drop against what it was, so find out what it was.\n- Assuming everyone has it, when it's one group.\n- A dip that's the season, a holiday or a one-off.\n- A change in how it's counted (a new tracking setup, a renamed event) that looks like a change in what people do.\n- Fixing how the page looks when the people leaving were never going to pay that price.\n\n## How to ask\n\n- Start from their words about the moment, then what happened, then what changed. Ask about the last time, never what people would do.\n- When it's a screen or a step of their app or website, ask once to see it (screens), in the order people go through it: a screenshot settles what words can't.\n- For someone new to this: one or two questions at a time, each with an example of a useful answer, in plain words.\n- When they don't know a number, don't ask for a guess: ask where it could be found, or how it could be counted from now on.\n- Likely causes come after \"what changed?\", never before: offer them as Qalam AI's hunches to rule in or out, never as their answer.\n- Say why a question matters when it isn't obvious, such as so a page isn't redesigned when the fee is what turns people away.\n\n## What to propose\n\nA diagnosis, not a fix:\n\n- The goal the step moves, worded as an outcome (more of the people who reach it get past it), under the business's money goal when there is one.\n- Two to four likely causes as problems, each worded as what goes wrong and for whom, and Assumed until something backs it.\n- Under each, the cheapest way to check it: compare their own records from before and after a change, ask a handful of people who left at that step why, count what the step records already, or watch a few people use it.\n- Which check to run first, and why that one.\n- Fixes only when they asked for them, or once a check has confirmed a cause."
