    "body": "## What to find out\n\nLook only for what bears on the problem they're working out. Leave the rest alone, however you'd design it.\n\nFrom the customer's side, on each screen:\n\n- Its one job: what someone came to this screen to do, and whether that's the first thing they see.\n- What's in the way of doing it: a step that could go, a field nobody needs, a control that doesn't look like what it does, two actions competing for the same press.\n- What's unclear at the moment of deciding: a total, a fee, a time, a condition, a word only the team uses.\n- What's missing at that moment: what someone needs to see to go on, such as the price with everything in it, when it arrives, what happens next, or how to undo it.\n- What pulls them away: a box that sends them off to look for something, a link out, a surprise.\n- Whether they can read it and reach it: contrast, size, a target too small, words cut off. Only where it bears on the problem.\n\nFrom the business's side:\n\n- What the screen earns from, and what it gives away: a discount shown where nobody needed it, a threshold almost nobody reaches.\n- What it costs when it goes wrong: people writing in, refunds, payments that fail.\n- Which number it moves, and how this screen moves it.\n\nAcross the screens, in the order they were shown:\n\n- Where something first appears that someone needed a screen earlier.\n- A step that repeats another, or could go.\n\n## What people usually miss\n\n- Fixing by adding: another banner, another line of help, another badge, when something already on the screen is in the way.\n- Several fixes on one screen at once: nobody can tell which one helped, and the screen gets louder each time.\n- Breaking what works: changing the part people use without trouble.\n- A cause that isn't on the screen at all: the price itself, a fee, a delivery time, stock. No layout fixes it; say so instead.\n- The screen before: where people leave is where it shows, not always where it starts.\n- Judging the screen by taste instead of by the problem.\n- Judging for one side: what helps customers can cut against what the business chose on purpose (its brand, a partner's place, a rule), and the other way round. Weigh each finding for both.\n- Assuming what the business needs: until it's known what the screens are for, who uses them and what it won't change, say what would need to be true instead of concluding.\n\n## How to ask\n\nHow to point at what you found and word it:\n\n- One finding per thing, worded as what goes wrong and for whom, in plain words.\n- Point at it by the words printed on the screen, exactly as they're printed, so it can be marked where it is. Where it has no words (a picture, a colour, a gap), say where on the screen it is.\n- Say in a sentence how it bears on the problem they're working out.\n- A finding from the business's side rests on how their business works, a goal or what they said: name which.\n- Rank them by what each costs the problem, the worst first, and leave out what doesn't bear on it.\n- Never quote a word or a number that isn't printed on the screens or in what they said.\n\n## What to propose\n\n- One change for each finding: the smallest that would fix it.\n- Swap, don't add: a change that puts something on a screen names what it replaces or takes away there. When nothing can go, propose another change, or none.\n- Prefer taking away, rewording or moving over adding.\n- Never a redesign or a new screen, and never two changes on the same thing.\n- Where the fix isn't on the screen (the price itself), say so and propose no change there.\n- Where the two sides pull apart and no change serves both, say what each side gets and leave the choice to them."
